(quasi-dependence)
	We say that the joint distribution $F_{XY}^{(w)}$ is quasi-independent with respect to the weight function ${w}$, if there exist density functions $\tilde{f_{X}}$ and $\tilde{f_{Y}}$, such that
\begin{equation} \label{eq:quasi_independence}
	F_{XY}^{(w)}(x,y) \propto \int\displaylimits_{-\infty}^{x}\int\displaylimits_{-\infty}^{y} {w}(s,t) \tilde{f_{X}}(s) \tilde{f_{Y}}(t) dt ds, \quad \forall x,y \in \mathbb{R} \: .
\end{equation}

Otherwise, we say that $F_{XY}^{(w)}$ is quasi-dependent with respect to the weight function ${w}$.
\label{def:quasi_independence}

\begin{equation} \label{eq:quasi_independence}
	F_{XY}^{(w)}(x,y) \propto \int\displaylimits_{-\infty}^{x}\int\displaylimits_{-\infty}^{y} {w}(s,t) \tilde{f_{X}}(s) \tilde{f_{Y}}(t) dt ds, \quad \forall x,y \in \mathbb{R} \: .
\end{equation}

Based on the sample $\mathcal{D} = \{(x_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^n$, we aim at performing the following hypothesis testing for quasi-dependence:
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{rl}
H_0: & F_{XY}^{(w)} \textrm{\; is \; quasi-independent} \\
H_1: & \textrm{otherwise}
\end{array}
\label{eq:main_hypothesis_testing}
\end{equation}

